  "collapse_recommendation": "Selection rule -> overall_1d (1d). The 11-skill latent abilities are highly collinear (max|r|=0.984), the first eigenvalue explains 94% of skill-ability variance, and held-out log-loss does not separate the structures beyond 1 SE. At N=52 the skills are not separable -> collapse toward a general-ability structure.",
  "aic_bic_caveat": "full_11d is AIC/BIC-best on the RAW table, but this is NOT evidence of 11 separable skills. Two artifacts inflate it: (1) full_11d is fit as the block-diagonal INDEPENDENT (identity-R) model, which gives each of the 52 persons 11 free per-skill abilities at the SAME nominal parameter count as 1D (no correlation parameters), so it absorbs more in-sample idiosyncrasy than one shared ability (loglik +1333 vs 1D at equal k) even though the measured inter-skill ability correlation is ~0.98; a correctly-specified CORRELATED 11-factor model would add 55 correlation parameters, is unidentifiable at N=52, and would collapse to ~1 factor. (2) The intermediate correlated 2-4d groupings use a coarse product Gauss-Hermite grid (5 nodes/dim; 5^4 max) while 1D and the independent 11D use 21 nodes/dim, so the collapsed models' marginal likelihood is under-resolved and their AIC/BIC is penalized relative to the two extremes. The HONEST, grid-fair arbiter is out-of-sample marginal log-loss, where full_11d (0.4776) beats 1D (0.4814) by 0.0038 -- ~5% of the +/-0.079 fold SE, i.e. no meaningful generalization gain. Combined with r~0.98, 94% first-eigenvalue variance, and a single Kaiser factor, the parsimonious, well-specified conclusion is UNIDIMENSIONAL collapse (overall_1d). The 11 task categories are reporting labels, not separable latent abilities at this bank size.",
